.TH dmd5620 1 "25 Nov 2026" "0.2.5" "AT&T DMD 5620 Terminal Emulator"
.SH NAME
dmd5620 \- AT&T DMD 5620 Terminal Emulator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dmd5620
[\fB\-\-firmware\fR \fI"VERSION"\fR]
[\fB\-\-nvram\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-scale\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-headless\fR]
[\fB\-\-name\fR \fINAME\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-mcp\fR]
[\fB\-\-shell\fR \fISHELL\fR |
\fB\-\-device\fR \fIDEVICE\fR [\fB\-\-baud\fR \fIN\fR] |
\fB\-\-telnet\fR \fIHOST\fR[:\fIPORT\fR] |
\fB\-\-tcp\fR \fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR]
.br
.B dmd5620 \-\-mcp\-bridge
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dmd5620
emulates the AT&T / Teletype DMD 5620 bitmap terminal, with its original
firmware, and supports the System V \fIlayers\fR protocol. It also has an
MCP server, so that an AI agent can see the screen and use the keyboard
and the mouse.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Displays help and exits.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Displays version and exits.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-firmware " " \fI"VERSION"\fR
Select the firmware version. \fI"VERSION"\fR is a string, and must
be one of either \fB"8;7;3"\fR or \fB"8;7;5"\fR. The default version
if not specified is \fB"8;7;5"\fR.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-nvram " " \fIFILE\fR
Store NVRAM state in file \fIFILE\fR. No default.
.TP
.BR \-\-scale " " \fIN\fR
Window size: 1, 2, 3 or 4. 1 is 800x1024, one DMD pixel per screen
point (the default); 2 is 1600x2048. The window has a fixed size: it
cannot be resized.
.TP
.BR \-\-headless
No window. The MCP tools still see the screen.
.TP
.BR \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
The name of this terminal in the MCP tools. The default is \fBdmd5620\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-mcp
Do not open the MCP control socket.
.TP
.BR \-\-mcp\-bridge
Run as the MCP server on standard input and output. It finds the running
emulators and passes each tool call to one of them.
.SH HOST CONNECTION
Give one of these. The default is a shell.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-shell " " \fISHELL\fR
Run the program \fISHELL\fR, e.g. "/bin/sh", on a pseudo terminal. The
default is \fB$SHELL\fR, else the login shell. The shell gets
\fBTERM=dmd\fR and a small clean environment. Not on Windows.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-inherit
Give the shell the whole environment of \fBdmd5620\fR.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-device " " \fIDEVICE\fR
Connect to the serial port \fIDEVICE\fR, e.g. "/dev/ttyS0" or "COM1".
.TP
.BR \-\-baud " " \fIN\fR
The speed of the serial port. The default is 9600.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-telnet " " \fIHOST\fR[:\fIPORT\fR]
Connect to a telnet server. The default port is 23. The terminal type is
\fBDMD\fR and the window size is 88x70.
.TP
.BR \-\-tcp " " \fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR
Connect to a raw TCP port, without the telnet protocol.
.SH KEYMAP
.TP
.BR F1\-F8
Terminal F1 through F8 keys
.TP
.BR F9
Terminal SETUP key
.TP
.BR SHIFT\+F9
Terminal RESET key
.TP
.BR Cmd\+V " (Mac), " Shift\+Insert ", " Ctrl\+Shift\+V
Type the text of the clipboard
.SH CONNECTING TO A SIMULATED 3B2
The OpenSIMH 3B2 simulator gives its serial lines as telnet ports. For
example, after \fBATTACH CONTTY 9100\fR in the simulator:
.P
.EX
$ dmd5620 \-\-telnet localhost:9100
.EE
.SH LAYERS ON THE LOCAL HOST
\fBlayers\fR, the host side of the DMD's window system, comes with dmd5620
on macOS, Linux and FreeBSD (github.com/tenox7/dmd-layers). When dmd5620
starts a shell and finds its \fIlayers\fR folder (next to the program, in
\fIContents/Resources\fR of DMD5620.app, or \fIPREFIX/lib/dmd5620\fR), it adds
\fIlayers/bin\fR to the shell's \fBPATH\fR and sets \fBDMDSYS\fR and
\fBDMD\fR, if they are not set.
.PP
Type \fBlayers\fR in the shell. Hold the right mouse button and choose
\fINew\fR, then sweep a rectangle with the right button to make a window.
Choose \fIExit\fR, or type \fBexit\fR in the last window, to stop layers.
Use firmware \fB8;7;5\fR and 19200 baud.
.PP
In a layers window, \fB32ld\fR \fIfile\fR loads a program into the DMD,
where it runs in that window. \fBdemo\fR lists the demos that come with
dmd5620 and \fBdemo\fR \fIname\fR runs one (most end with \fBq\fR).
\fBlens\fR, \fBhp2621\fR and \fBtek4014\fR are also there
(github.com/tenox7/dev5620).
.SH USING SVR3 LAYERS
One of the primary features of dmd5620 is support for the System V Release 3
\fIlayers\fR program. Real DMD5620 terminals were hard-wired directly
to hosts using serial ports, and have no notion of networks.
.PP
In order to use dmd5620 with a networked System V Release 3 over a network,
either using \fItelnet\fR or \fIssh\fR, you must enable "encoding" on the
terminal.
.PP
To enable encoding, first press F9 (the \fISETUP\fR key) and then select "Host
Options", either with the mouse or by pressing F2. Next, select toggle
"Encoding Off" to "Encoding On" either with the mouse or by pressing F3.
.PP
"Encoding" will ensure that telnet and SSH control characters are properly
escaped.
.SH SAVING NVRAM
All terminal settings are forgotten on shutdown. To save terminal
settings, the contents of NVRAM must be stored in a file using
the \fB\-\-nvram\fR flag.
.PP
Without an NVRAM file, or with a new one, the terminal starts with the
firmware's defaults, but with Port A at 19200 baud, not 1200.
.PP
\fINote that the NVRAM format is different between firmware versions!\fR
Using the same NVRAM file on an \fB8;7;5\fR firmware terminal
and an \fB8;7;3\fR firmware terminal is NOT SUPPORTED and will result
in undesirable behavior!
.SH KNOWN ISSUES
.IP \(bu 3
System V Release 3 \fIlayers\fR does not work when using Telnet to connect to a
real or simulated 3B2 using AT&T/WINS TCP/IP.  This is a limitation of the
AT&T/WINS TCP/IP package, and not of the DMD 5620 terminal emulator. This can
be worked around by connecting to a simulated serial port (e.g. Console,
Contty, or PORTS) listening for network connections.
.RE
